1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
It's been called the throne room, the library, and a bunch of other names we can't say.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
But whatever you call it, we can't live without it.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:17,000
From tub to toilet to toothpaste, here's everything you ever wanted to know about the most used and least discussed room in your house.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, bathroom tech on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
They say a man's home is his castle, and at the nucleus of

6
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:44,000
that castle is a tile and porcelain sanctuary.

7
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:48,000
We go there when nature calls, when we need to clean up, and

8
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:52,000
when we need to escape. The central component of most

9
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:56,000
bathrooms is the tub, and for more than 100 years, they've

10
00:00:56,001 --> 00:01:00,500
they've pretty much been made the same way.

11
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:03,940
At the Kohler factory in Kohler, Wisconsin, every day,

12
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:05,840
thousands of tubs are created.

13
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:10,640
Their basic ingredients, steel and enamel.

14
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:13,660
The bathtub begins in a vat of molten metal.

15
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:16,460
The liquid steel is then poured into earthen frames

16
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:18,960
and allowed to cool.

17
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:22,660
Once the frame is removed, the steel is buffed out by hand

18
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:25,960
and then heat it to 1,700 degrees.

19
00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:29,360
When it's literally red hot, layers of enamel powder,

20
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:32,740
essentially ground glass, are sprinkled over all of the tub's

21
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:34,740
visible parts.

22
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,680
The heat melts the glass and causes it

23
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:38,980
to adhere to the metal.

24
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:42,240
A gleaming fixture results.

25
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:44,980
Just add water, and you're ready to enjoy.

26
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,860
But before there were tubs and even plumbing,

27
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:49,160
things were a bit different.

28
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:55,560
Primitive humans doubtlessly sought out streams and ponds

29
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:58,600
in which to clean themselves.

30
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:02,000
But once these hunter-gatherers began to settle down into cities,

31
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:04,400
they had to develop ways to bring the bathwater to them.

32
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:11,920
It began in the Indus Valley in modern-day Pakistan.

33
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:16,520
In 2500 BC, India's ancient ancestors, the Harapans,

34
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:20,020
created the forerunner of the modern bathroom.

35
00:02:20,021 --> 00:02:22,920
The Harrapins were famous for their organization

36
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:24,820
of their houses.

37
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:27,620
They were the first, really, to lay out cities,

38
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:32,500
and in those cities, to take into account waste management.

39
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,400
Virtually every Harrapan home had a primitive shower

40
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,000
stall with drains where they could douse themselves

41
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:41,140
with buckets of water.

42
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:43,680
They did, however, lack an efficient water delivery

43
00:02:43,780 --> 00:02:44,940
system.

44
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:47,140
The water had to be drawn from nearby wells

45
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,180
and carried into the home.

46
00:02:49,181 --> 00:02:52,080
That water was used for the baths and for flushing

47
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:55,980
their toilets, and it would all drain into a central sewer

48
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,080
system.

49
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:00,720
And that sewer system would, in turn, take the water and dump

50
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:03,020
it into the nearby river.

51
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:04,820
But running water as we know it

52
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:08,240
was conceived by the Greeks.

53
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:11,940
Buried beneath their cities was a system of terracotta pipes

54
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:13,880
linked to local rivers and springs.

55
00:03:13,881 --> 00:03:19,480
Springs. The Greeks developed the building

56
00:03:19,481 --> 00:03:26,120
of aqueducts to bring fresh water into their cities, both to fountain houses where people could go and

57
00:03:26,121 --> 00:03:32,200
get drinking water, and especially to the gymnasium so that you'd have running water for the bathing

58
00:03:32,201 --> 00:03:36,280
after exercise. A Greek bath lacked two things we

59
00:03:36,281 --> 00:03:42,520
take for granted, hot water and soap. What they would do is they would take a

60
00:03:42,521 --> 00:03:47,520
metal implement called a strigil and scrape either themselves

61
00:03:47,521 --> 00:03:51,520
or a friend or more likely a slave, scrape all the caked

62
00:03:51,521 --> 00:03:56,520
dirt off, and then wash.

63
00:03:56,521 --> 00:03:59,520
The Romans took up where the Greeks left off,

64
00:03:59,521 --> 00:04:02,520
installing an aqueduct system beneath the ground that stretched

65
00:04:02,521 --> 00:04:04,520
almost 200 miles.

66
00:04:04,521 --> 00:04:07,520
Water flowed from distant mountain streams all the way

67
00:04:07,521 --> 00:04:09,520
to Rome.

68
00:04:09,521 --> 00:04:12,720
Today, all that remains of the system are ruins of the bridges

69
00:04:12,721 --> 00:04:15,760
that supported the above-ground stretches of pipe.

70
00:04:15,761 --> 00:04:18,760
As the Romans became more familiar with Greek culture

71
00:04:18,761 --> 00:04:21,560
in the late 3rd and 2nd centuries BC,

72
00:04:21,561 --> 00:04:26,280
they picked up from the Greeks the idea of public baths.

73
00:04:26,281 --> 00:04:29,080
The abundance of water flowing through the aqueducts

74
00:04:29,081 --> 00:04:32,080
enabled Roman engineers to construct the greatest public

75
00:04:32,081 --> 00:04:35,680
bathhouses the world had ever known.

76
00:04:35,681 --> 00:04:38,680
The baths were so large, it's hard for us to conceive of them.

77
00:04:38,681 --> 00:04:42,780
They could handle 2,500 men or women in the women's bath

78
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:47,420
in a single day, and you would go for the entire day.

79
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:51,620
The water in the bathing pools was hot.

80
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:55,400
Directly below were massive furnace rooms burning wood

81
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:56,940
and coal.

82
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,800
The flames scorching the stone floors of bathhouses

83
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:03,340
were powerful enough to fill the rooms with sauna-like heat.

84
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:06,200
Bathing did become an almost universal phenomenon

85
00:05:06,201 --> 00:05:07,760
phenomenon throughout the empire,

86
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:10,140
thanks to the building of bathing facilities

87
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:13,640
in virtually every Roman town.

88
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:16,240
But when the Roman Empire finally fell,

89
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:19,880
bathroom technology went with it.

90
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:22,620
Without the Roman aqueducts, fresh water would once again

91
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:24,760
have to be lugged from wells.

92
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:26,620
It's no wonder that bathing fell out of favor

93
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:29,160
for more than 10 centuries.

94
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:30,600
What people don't appreciate today

95
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:32,900
is during long periods, during the Middle Ages,

96
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,600
even into the Renaissance, people

97
00:05:34,601 --> 00:05:39,300
People would bathe maybe once a year, and that was considered all that was necessary.

98
00:05:39,301 --> 00:05:45,800
In the 18th century, bathing began a long overdue comeback in Europe.

99
00:05:45,801 --> 00:05:51,840
Fancy new copper tubs became all the rage, some shaped like giant boots.

100
00:05:51,841 --> 00:05:55,660
Benjamin Franklin raised America's awareness when he brought one back with him after visiting

101
00:05:55,661 --> 00:05:56,660
France.

102
00:05:56,661 --> 00:06:04,580
Early versions of the bathtub's cousin, the shower, began to appear in the 18th century.

103
00:06:04,581 --> 00:06:08,580
water flowed down from an elevated tank through the

104
00:06:08,581 --> 00:06:12,580
shower head, as long as the limited supply of water lasted.

105
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:19,580
Bathroom tech could go only so far until engineers could construct

106
00:06:19,581 --> 00:06:23,580
water delivery systems able to service entire cities.

107
00:06:23,581 --> 00:06:27,580
In 1842, New York City paved the way.

108
00:06:27,581 --> 00:06:31,580
The system's designers harnessed a fundamental law of nature,

109
00:06:31,581 --> 00:06:34,920
flows downhill. Your city's system follows the

110
00:06:34,921 --> 00:06:38,580
same principle. Water is pumped to the top of

111
00:06:38,581 --> 00:06:42,580
giant towers that are linked to pipes beneath the streets.

112
00:06:42,581 --> 00:06:45,580
Since the tower is higher than the water's final destination,

113
00:06:45,581 --> 00:06:49,580
gravity maintains pressure and forces the water through pipes

114
00:06:49,581 --> 00:06:56,580
to your tap. After water is used,

115
00:06:56,581 --> 00:07:00,580
gravity once again carries it away through sewer pipes angled

116
00:07:00,581 --> 00:07:05,420
pipes angled downhill. During the 19th century, more and

117
00:07:05,421 --> 00:07:09,880
more cities followed New York's example. One turn of a handle

118
00:07:09,881 --> 00:07:13,820
brought water into bathrooms. The only problem is that it was

119
00:07:13,821 --> 00:07:17,540
all cold. Some bathtub makers trying to solve

120
00:07:17,541 --> 00:07:21,540
the problem created models that endangered their customers.

121
00:07:21,541 --> 00:07:25,340
They were heated in a terrifying way, particularly

122
00:07:25,341 --> 00:07:29,540
the General Gordon gas bath, which was a flame, a naked flame

123
00:07:29,541 --> 00:07:32,180
beneath the tin bath that scorched the bottom off

124
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:33,140
your behind.

125
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:38,580
In 1889, Pittsburgh entrepreneur Edwin Rood

126
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:42,380
invented a more practical alternative, the home hot water

127
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:44,280
heater.

128
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:47,300
It used coils of copper pipe stacked atop a small gas

129
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:48,700
furnace.

130
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:52,300
Water passing through the copper pipes was quickly heated.

131
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:55,200
Once it left the heater, the hot water could then be piped

132
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:58,440
directly to sinks, tubs, and showers.

133
00:07:58,441 --> 00:08:02,140
It made it very convenient, certainly, to have a warm bath,

134
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:04,980
which was obviously more pleasant than a cold or tepid

135
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:05,880
bath.

136
00:08:05,980 --> 00:08:08,820
And the ability to drain the water away without the use of

137
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:12,160
servants also made it more accessible to everybody.

138
00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:15,060
During the 20th century, engineers developed the

139
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:20,800
modern design, in which water is heated inside a steel drum.

140
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:23,860
The newer models were outfitted with critical safety devices,

141
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:27,640
such as metal plugs, designed to melt and release the water if

142
00:08:27,641 --> 00:08:30,440
if the temperature rose dangerously high.

143
00:08:30,441 --> 00:08:36,440
Sometimes they didn't. The superheated water inside

144
00:08:36,441 --> 00:08:39,940
the tank would explode, sometimes injuring or killing unsuspecting

145
00:08:39,941 --> 00:08:44,640
homeowners. The Watts Regulator Company

146
00:08:44,641 --> 00:08:48,640
solved the problem in the 1930s. Its engineers developed an

147
00:08:48,641 --> 00:08:52,140
automatic temperature and pressure safety release valve,

148
00:08:52,141 --> 00:08:55,140
which allowed pent-up heat to vent before reaching critical

149
00:08:55,141 --> 00:09:05,140
mass. Throughout the 20th century,

150
00:09:05,141 --> 00:09:08,140
the American bathroom continued to evolve.

151
00:09:08,141 --> 00:09:12,140
At first, they were almost impractically small.

152
00:09:12,141 --> 00:09:15,140
By the 1950s, architects were designing larger, more

153
00:09:15,141 --> 00:09:18,140
comfortable spaces. But they often forced families

154
00:09:18,141 --> 00:09:24,140
to share just one bathroom. The bathroom would evolve

155
00:09:24,141 --> 00:09:26,880
would evolve still further with the arrival of the master bath

156
00:09:26,980 --> 00:09:28,380
in the 1970s.

157
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:32,140
Bathrooms began to be bigger, and walls came down.

158
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:35,520
It was very typical to see a big whirlpool tub right in the middle

159
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:36,760
of a master bedroom.

160
00:09:36,860 --> 00:09:37,860
It was an experience.

161
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:40,560
It was the sort of the experience generation

162
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:43,300
when more was more.

163
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:45,760
The size of the new whirlpool baths,

164
00:09:45,860 --> 00:09:48,200
and not to mention the weight of all that water,

165
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:51,000
required new tub-making technologies.

166
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:53,660
Traditional steel and enamel tubs were out,

167
00:09:53,661 --> 00:09:57,860
and heavy-duty plastics were in.

168
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:01,500
To make a Kohler Vicryl tub, a bundle of plastic material

169
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:04,740
called a load is placed inside a reverse mold

170
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:06,840
of the finished tub.

171
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:09,240
Once the mold closes, the tub is literally

172
00:10:09,340 --> 00:10:10,140
pressed into shape.

173
00:10:13,580 --> 00:10:15,240
After it's removed from the mold,

174
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:19,960
the tub is cleaned and outfitted with water jets.

175
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:22,820
The daily cleanliness is no longer a question.

176
00:10:22,821 --> 00:10:26,360
It's something that has become accessible to everyone.

177
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:30,360
But it's just how much pampering can you pack into that room

178
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:34,760
is the true measure of luxury in today's bathrooms.

179
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:39,360
But relaxing isn't the only thing we do in the bathroom.

180
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:44,580
Next, we'll crack the veneer on the Porcelain God.

181
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:46,920
At its peak, Rome's system of aqueducts

182
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:51,020
supplied 300 gallons of fresh water a day for each citizen,

183
00:10:51,021 --> 00:10:54,440
Almost the amount that an American family of four uses today.

184
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:57,800
Bathroom Tech will continue on Modern Marvels.

185
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:04,980
You're watching Bathroom Tech on Modern Marvels.

186
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:10,680
This porcelain wonder is the nucleus of every bathroom.

187
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:17,400
Every day in America, we flush down at our own waste and 7 billion gallons of water.

188
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:20,840
Most of us don't give the toilet a second thought.

189
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:22,820
until something goes wrong with it.

190
00:11:22,821 --> 00:11:24,480
But the Japanese are transforming it

191
00:11:24,481 --> 00:11:26,980
into a high-tech art form.

192
00:11:26,981 --> 00:11:29,620
Their number one toilet manufacturer, Toto,

193
00:11:29,621 --> 00:11:32,320
has come out with several different designs,

194
00:11:32,321 --> 00:11:36,620
basically incorporating the bidet in with the toilet

195
00:11:36,621 --> 00:11:38,780
so that as you're going to the bathroom,

196
00:11:38,781 --> 00:11:41,720
you can push one button and an arm swings out

197
00:11:41,721 --> 00:11:44,420
and shoots water up on your rump.

198
00:11:44,421 --> 00:11:46,820
You push another button and it'll shoot hot air

199
00:11:46,821 --> 00:11:48,520
to dry off your bottom.

200
00:11:48,521 --> 00:11:51,020
And they also have heat warming seats.

201
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:53,820
It can be a problem, though, if you sit down

202
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:57,860
and you push the wrong button at the wrong time.

203
00:11:57,960 --> 00:12:00,260
For most Americans, the functional white commode

204
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:02,060
is still the standard.

205
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:03,760
And by any standard, the modern toilet

206
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:05,780
is a spectacularly successful invention.

207
00:12:08,940 --> 00:12:11,720
It's durable, doesn't require a power supply,

208
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:15,820
and it rarely, if ever, breaks down.

209
00:12:15,821 --> 00:12:19,120
Each and every toilet begins as a liquid clay mixture,

210
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:22,020
called slip.

211
00:12:22,120 --> 00:12:26,860
The slip is poured into molds and allowed to harden.

212
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:29,760
Once it's removed from the mold, the toilet's clay surface

213
00:12:29,860 --> 00:12:31,960
must be smoothed out by hand.

214
00:12:32,060 --> 00:12:33,880
Any imperfection will be plainly visible

215
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:37,280
in the finished product.

216
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:41,480
Next, robotic arms spray the toilet body with glaze paint,

217
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:45,820
and it's baked for 36 hours inside a huge industrial kiln.

218
00:12:45,821 --> 00:12:47,960
After the toilet is removed from the kiln,

219
00:12:47,961 --> 00:12:49,820
it exhibits the familiar porcelain veneer.

220
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:55,200
But before the toilet, bathroom sanitation

221
00:12:55,201 --> 00:12:58,740
was primitive at best.

222
00:12:58,741 --> 00:13:00,400
While the Harapons and the Minoans

223
00:13:00,401 --> 00:13:02,100
used rudimentary forms of plumbing

224
00:13:02,101 --> 00:13:05,300
to flush away their waste, elsewhere in the ancient world,

225
00:13:05,301 --> 00:13:08,280
bathroom technology consisted of clay pots.

226
00:13:08,281 --> 00:13:10,580
I don't know if the Greeks invented the chamber pot,

227
00:13:10,581 --> 00:13:12,080
but they certainly used it.

228
00:13:12,081 --> 00:13:15,180
There are numerous attestations to it.

229
00:13:15,181 --> 00:13:19,520
wealthy men would even have a slave who was called the

230
00:13:19,521 --> 00:13:22,920
lasanophoros, the chamber pot carrier, whose job it was to

231
00:13:22,921 --> 00:13:27,820
come in and remove the chamber pot and clean it out.

232
00:13:27,821 --> 00:13:30,620
The Romans put the chamber pot carrier out of business with

233
00:13:30,621 --> 00:13:33,520
the development of public latrines, which were essentially

234
00:13:33,521 --> 00:13:36,440
seat holes carved into stone benches.

235
00:13:36,441 --> 00:13:40,380
You sit down over one of the holes, and under the hole is a

236
00:13:40,381 --> 00:13:43,540
trench with water running through it.

237
00:13:43,541 --> 00:13:49,180
And so whatever you deposit into the hole falls into the running

238
00:13:49,181 --> 00:13:53,280
water and gets swept away immediately.

239
00:13:53,281 --> 00:13:56,080
The Romans also innovated the precursor of another bathroom

240
00:13:56,081 --> 00:13:58,780
technology, the pay toilet.

241
00:13:58,781 --> 00:14:01,900
The latrines were initially provided free of charge,

242
00:14:01,901 --> 00:14:06,240
but the emperor Vespasian began to raise funds from the use of

243
00:14:06,241 --> 00:14:09,000
latrines.

244
00:14:09,001 --> 00:14:12,140
But taxing the toilets couldn't save the Roman Empire.

245
00:14:12,141 --> 00:14:14,140
And with its fall, toilet technology

246
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:16,840
would be set back for centuries.

247
00:14:16,940 --> 00:14:19,740
The only thing that you had indoors for the next, really,

248
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:22,980
1,000 years was the chamber pot, which was really something

249
00:14:23,080 --> 00:14:24,180
of a horror story.

250
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:26,380
It was a convenience in one way when you needed to go in the

251
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:32,100
middle of the night, but then who emptied it in the morning?

252
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:34,100
Some people didn't even wait for morning.

253
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:38,600
At nighttime was the time when people would dump the contents

254
00:14:38,601 --> 00:14:43,900
of this chamber pot outside their windows into the streets

255
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,100
below.

256
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:49,740
And the idea that a man walks on the left side of the female

257
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:51,240
dates back to this time.

258
00:14:51,340 --> 00:14:54,380
It was polite for him to get hit by the contents of the chamber

259
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:58,960
pot and to spare the woman.

260
00:14:59,060 --> 00:15:00,960
People often took their chamber pots with them

261
00:15:01,060 --> 00:15:02,220
when they traveled.

262
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:06,020
Pots could also be rented for parties or even pit stops.

263
00:15:06,021 --> 00:15:10,860
Another rather intriguing figure of medieval sanitary history

264
00:15:10,861 --> 00:15:14,420
is the human laboratory, who was a man who'd go along

265
00:15:14,421 --> 00:15:16,560
with a great black cloak down the streets,

266
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:19,540
holding a stool and a chamber pot in each hand.

267
00:15:19,980 --> 00:15:22,120
And when you paid him a penny, he'd put the stool down

268
00:15:22,121 --> 00:15:23,080
with a chamber pot on it.

269
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:26,100
You would sit on it, and whilst you're relieving yourself

270
00:15:26,101 --> 00:15:29,800
of your troubles, he would surround you with his black cloak,

271
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:31,800
and then you'd get up and go on your way.

272
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:35,520
In the 16th century, flush toilet technology

273
00:15:35,521 --> 00:15:38,160
made its debut in England.

274
00:15:38,161 --> 00:15:42,660
The first nearly modern toilet was made for Queen Elizabeth

275
00:15:42,661 --> 00:15:45,320
the First in 1596.

276
00:15:45,321 --> 00:15:48,420
It was made by her godson, Sir John Harrington.

277
00:15:48,421 --> 00:15:50,420
He made it to get back in her good graces

278
00:15:50,421 --> 00:15:53,120
because she had banished him from court

279
00:15:53,121 --> 00:15:55,280
for using foul language.

280
00:15:55,281 --> 00:15:57,120
He came up with a really clever device.

281
00:15:57,121 --> 00:15:58,520
It had a tank at the top.

282
00:15:58,521 --> 00:16:01,480
It had a valve you opened to let water down.

283
00:16:01,481 --> 00:16:04,320
And there was a trap door that you could close

284
00:16:04,321 --> 00:16:07,480
after you used the toilet.

285
00:16:07,481 --> 00:16:11,160
Harrington's primitive toilet had a critical design flaw.

286
00:16:11,161 --> 00:16:13,560
The pipe beneath the bowl was vertical.

287
00:16:13,561 --> 00:16:17,260
Waste went straight down, and sewer smells came straight up.

288
00:16:17,261 --> 00:16:20,880
The queen complained that fumes came up from the cesspool,

289
00:16:20,881 --> 00:16:24,880
but it was a problem that her godson was never able to solve.

290
00:16:24,881 --> 00:16:29,080
What he did foolishly is he wrote a book on what he called

291
00:16:29,081 --> 00:16:31,680
her privy imperfection, her toilet,

292
00:16:31,681 --> 00:16:34,180
revealing not only how the toilet worked,

293
00:16:34,280 --> 00:16:36,680
but some personal secrets about the queen,

294
00:16:36,780 --> 00:16:41,180
which incurred her ire, and she banished him from court again.

295
00:16:42,520 --> 00:16:44,980
After Harrington's toilet got the royal flush,

296
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:48,180
toilet technology continued to remain low-tech,

297
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:49,540
and even no-tech.

298
00:16:50,380 --> 00:16:52,100
You realize how bad the situation was

299
00:16:52,101 --> 00:16:53,520
if you look at the Palace of Versailles.

300
00:16:54,020 --> 00:16:56,160
A fortune was spent in constructing it.

301
00:16:56,161 --> 00:16:57,880
It had these wonderful hall of mirrors,

302
00:16:58,020 --> 00:16:59,420
elaborate chandeliers,

303
00:16:59,421 --> 00:17:02,800
and you might have 1,000 people being entertained,

304
00:17:02,801 --> 00:17:04,760
eating and drinking copiously,

305
00:17:04,761 --> 00:17:06,720
but where did they go to the bathroom?

306
00:17:06,721 --> 00:17:08,160
There was not a single bathroom

307
00:17:08,161 --> 00:17:09,860
in the entire elaborate palace.

308
00:17:09,861 --> 00:17:12,200
And the answer is, they went in the stairwells.

309
00:17:12,201 --> 00:17:14,760
One of the reasons the French applied so much perfume

310
00:17:14,761 --> 00:17:18,300
during that period was to overcome all of the indoor odors

311
00:17:18,301 --> 00:17:21,540
from people relieving themselves.

312
00:17:21,541 --> 00:17:23,080
Outside Versailles,

313
00:17:23,081 --> 00:17:26,180
people were relieving themselves in indoor cesspits.

314
00:17:26,181 --> 00:17:28,720
They were simply benches or seats perched over holes

315
00:17:28,721 --> 00:17:32,720
with wood, stone, or brick. Their main drawback, aside from

316
00:17:32,721 --> 00:17:36,720
the smell, was that you had to pay night soil men or rakers

317
00:17:36,721 --> 00:17:40,720
to clean them out. In the 18th century, American

318
00:17:40,721 --> 00:17:44,720
colonists modified the cesspit by taking it outside and

319
00:17:44,721 --> 00:17:46,720
constructing a small wooden shack over it.

320
00:17:46,721 --> 00:17:50,720
The outhouse was born. In 1775, while America was

321
00:17:50,721 --> 00:17:54,720
embroiled in the Revolutionary War, back in the mother country,

322
00:17:54,721 --> 00:17:57,180
Back in the mother country, another revolution was taking

323
00:17:57,181 --> 00:18:01,960
place, this time in toilet technology.

324
00:18:01,961 --> 00:18:04,760
British watchmaker Alexander Cummings invented a toilet

325
00:18:04,761 --> 00:18:07,160
with a twist, literally.

326
00:18:07,161 --> 00:18:10,560
The pipe beneath Cummings' toilet bowl curved backward

327
00:18:10,561 --> 00:18:12,760
in a distinctive U-shaped bend.

328
00:18:12,761 --> 00:18:14,800
This allowed water to pool in the pipe,

329
00:18:14,801 --> 00:18:18,060
cutting off smells from below.

330
00:18:18,061 --> 00:18:19,780
It actually is the modern toilet,

331
00:18:19,781 --> 00:18:23,120
because we still have that water separating us

332
00:18:23,121 --> 00:18:28,660
from the cesspool today. Over the next 100 years,

333
00:18:28,661 --> 00:18:31,720
several inventors improved on Cumming's design.

334
00:18:31,721 --> 00:18:35,360
The key innovation was a water siphoning system to force waste

335
00:18:35,361 --> 00:18:39,880
through the base of the bowl with unparalleled efficiency.

336
00:18:39,881 --> 00:18:42,280
What worked then still works now.

337
00:18:45,580 --> 00:18:48,980
Once the toilet's flush handle is pulled, a valve inside the

338
00:18:48,981 --> 00:18:52,880
holding tank called the flapper opens up.

339
00:18:52,881 --> 00:18:55,380
The water drains quickly into the bowl through a series of

340
00:18:55,381 --> 00:19:00,580
angled holes under the rim. The swirling water exits down

341
00:19:00,581 --> 00:19:03,620
the waste pipe past the U-shaped bend, creating a

342
00:19:03,621 --> 00:19:08,620
siphoning effect that pulls the water from the bowl.

343
00:19:08,621 --> 00:19:11,040
The man who's often credited with inventing this flushing

344
00:19:11,041 --> 00:19:15,740
wonder probably had little to do with it, Thomas Crapper.

345
00:19:15,741 --> 00:19:18,940
Yes, he really existed. However, the Victorian-era

346
00:19:18,941 --> 00:19:21,680
plumbing magnate nevertheless earned his place in toilet

347
00:19:21,681 --> 00:19:25,020
history, if only by selling lots of them.

348
00:19:25,120 --> 00:19:28,320
During World War I, when American soldiers were

349
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:32,360
stationed over in Britain, they would come across a lot of these

350
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:36,400
toilets, and they started the euphemism of,

351
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:39,800
I'm going to the Crapper, and they based it on what they saw

352
00:19:39,900 --> 00:19:44,200
on the toilets, which said Thomas Crapper and company.

353
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:46,380
While Crapper was making a name for himself,

354
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:49,400
two enterprising brothers were busy inventing the toilet's most

355
00:19:49,401 --> 00:19:53,400
Next, everything you always wanted to know about toilet paper

356
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:55,400
but were afraid to ask.

357
00:19:57,440 --> 00:19:59,400
During medieval times,

358
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:01,400
castle dwellers strengthened their defenses

359
00:20:01,440 --> 00:20:03,400
by dumping waste into the moat.

360
00:20:03,440 --> 00:20:06,400
The raw sewage discouraged invaders from crossing.

361
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:09,400
Bathroom Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

362
00:20:09,401 --> 00:20:12,160
Bathroom Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

363
00:20:18,440 --> 00:20:21,860
You're watching Bathroom Tech on Modern Marvels.

364
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:27,560
With every year, bathroom technology

365
00:20:27,561 --> 00:20:29,900
is more and more about satisfying our needs

366
00:20:29,901 --> 00:20:31,140
for comfort and convenience.

367
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:34,700
But beginning in the 1990s, toilet manufacturers

368
00:20:34,701 --> 00:20:37,000
adopted a new priority, the environment.

369
00:20:37,001 --> 00:20:41,000
engineers had to respond to strict new water conservation laws.

370
00:20:41,001 --> 00:20:44,000
Up until that time, the average toilet used three and a half

371
00:20:44,001 --> 00:20:46,000
gallons per flush.

372
00:20:46,001 --> 00:20:51,000
They had to invent more efficient models that used only 1.6.

373
00:20:51,001 --> 00:20:54,000
They eventually developed improved water channels to help

374
00:20:54,001 --> 00:20:56,000
increase the velocity of the water.

375
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:01,000
One American standard low flush toilet is so powerful that it's

376
00:21:01,001 --> 00:21:06,000
been demonstrated to clear 11 golf balls in a single flush.

377
00:21:06,001 --> 00:21:10,000
Of course, the item most commonly flushed isn't golf

378
00:21:10,001 --> 00:21:11,000
balls.

379
00:21:11,001 --> 00:21:14,000
It's toilet paper, which, as you might expect,

380
00:21:14,001 --> 00:21:16,000
had a long and winding history.

381
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:23,000
Although the Chinese invented paper in the second century,

382
00:21:23,001 --> 00:21:26,000
it took them more than 1,200 years to get around to using it

383
00:21:26,001 --> 00:21:27,000
in the bathroom.

384
00:21:27,001 --> 00:21:31,000
They finally did so in 1391 AD, but it was strictly for the use

385
00:21:31,001 --> 00:21:33,000
of emperors.

386
00:21:33,001 --> 00:21:35,400
And what did that leave commoners?

387
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:37,400
People generally used their hands.

388
00:21:37,500 --> 00:21:40,400
And currently, in many countries around the world

389
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:43,240
where paper is a premium, people continue

390
00:21:43,340 --> 00:21:45,040
to use their left hand.

391
00:21:45,140 --> 00:21:48,740
That is why when you travel to parts of the Middle East,

392
00:21:48,840 --> 00:21:52,160
Southeast Asia, and Asia, you won't find any left-handed

393
00:21:52,260 --> 00:21:53,060
people.

394
00:21:53,160 --> 00:21:56,060
Everyone there is right-handed because the left hand is

395
00:21:56,160 --> 00:21:59,100
considered unclean.

396
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:01,800
In medieval Europe, commoners used hay, grass,

397
00:22:01,801 --> 00:22:04,200
plant leaves to clean themselves.

398
00:22:04,300 --> 00:22:07,200
In early America, millions used corn cobs.

399
00:22:07,300 --> 00:22:11,440
The cobs were softened first by prolonged soaking in water.

400
00:22:11,540 --> 00:22:16,140
The corn cobs were generally given to the pigs to eat.

401
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:17,740
And then when the pigs were finished with them

402
00:22:17,840 --> 00:22:20,560
and there was just the cob left, they would take those

403
00:22:20,660 --> 00:22:22,760
and use them to wipe themselves.

404
00:22:22,860 --> 00:22:26,360
So there was very little waste.

405
00:22:26,460 --> 00:22:28,860
When mass-published newspapers and catalogs

406
00:22:28,861 --> 00:22:31,500
became commonplace in the 19th century,

407
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:34,260
Americans finally said goodbye to the corn cobs,

408
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:37,500
and hello to Sears and Roebuck.

409
00:22:37,600 --> 00:22:41,600
People would take the catalog, hang it in their outhouses,

410
00:22:41,700 --> 00:22:45,240
and they would read from it while they were doing their business.

411
00:22:45,340 --> 00:22:46,740
And at the finish of the business,

412
00:22:46,840 --> 00:22:50,440
they would tear off a piece and use it to wipe themselves.

413
00:22:50,540 --> 00:22:53,080
But things changed in the 1920s.

414
00:22:53,180 --> 00:22:56,960
Unfortunately, Sears started using glossy print paper

415
00:22:56,961 --> 00:23:00,960
The absorbing benefits of the catalog kind of lost it.

416
00:23:01,060 --> 00:23:04,360
So you didn't see so many people using the Sears catalog

417
00:23:04,460 --> 00:23:08,100
as toilet paper from then on.

418
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:09,900
By that time, however, consumers

419
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:15,020
had another option, real toilet paper.

420
00:23:15,120 --> 00:23:16,920
The idea of selling paper for that purpose

421
00:23:17,020 --> 00:23:19,320
had been around since before the Civil War.

422
00:23:19,420 --> 00:23:21,520
There was a man named Joseph Gaietti.

423
00:23:21,620 --> 00:23:24,720
He was a New Yorker, and he had a paper business in New Jersey.

424
00:23:24,721 --> 00:23:27,720
He was the first person who actually took paper,

425
00:23:27,820 --> 00:23:29,720
cut it into sheets, into small sheets,

426
00:23:29,820 --> 00:23:34,720
and sold it through drug stores as therapeutic paper.

427
00:23:34,820 --> 00:23:36,720
But the product flopped.

428
00:23:36,820 --> 00:23:39,720
The people who bought them thought the paper was too nice

429
00:23:39,820 --> 00:23:41,720
and ended up using it as stationery, writing on it,

430
00:23:41,820 --> 00:23:44,720
and still using their catalog.

431
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:48,720
In 1879, entrepreneurs Irvin and Clarence Scott

432
00:23:48,820 --> 00:23:51,720
began selling rolled toilet paper.

433
00:23:51,820 --> 00:23:54,720
It was made from tissue paper, bought from other metals,

434
00:23:54,721 --> 00:23:58,720
which they cut up, rolled, and repackaged.

435
00:23:58,820 --> 00:24:01,120
Although there have been some improvements over the years,

436
00:24:01,220 --> 00:24:04,120
today's toilet tissue is made basically the same way.

437
00:24:07,060 --> 00:24:09,660
Like all paper, toilet tissue begins life as a tree.

438
00:24:14,380 --> 00:24:16,980
Trees are logged and then processed at pulp mills,

439
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:19,680
where the wood chips are ground up and mixed with chemicals

440
00:24:19,780 --> 00:24:21,180
and water.

441
00:24:21,280 --> 00:24:23,680
The concoction has the consistency of oatmeal.

442
00:24:23,681 --> 00:24:26,020
oatmeal.

443
00:24:26,120 --> 00:24:28,680
The wood pulp mixture is then fed into the Fordriner

444
00:24:28,780 --> 00:24:30,720
papermaking machine.

445
00:24:30,820 --> 00:24:32,920
If you can take five trailer trucks

446
00:24:33,020 --> 00:24:37,620
and put them in a bundle, that's how big the machines are.

447
00:24:37,720 --> 00:24:39,500
In the Fordriner, the pulp is laid

448
00:24:39,600 --> 00:24:42,940
onto large screens and air-dried with heaters.

449
00:24:43,040 --> 00:24:46,200
Once a section of paper is dry, it's rolled forward,

450
00:24:46,300 --> 00:24:49,580
and more pulp is added to the screens.

451
00:24:49,680 --> 00:24:53,680
In this way, a continuous roll of paper is produced.

452
00:24:53,681 --> 00:24:58,280
These rolls of paper, called parent rolls, are massive.

453
00:24:58,281 --> 00:25:02,220
At the Angel Soft Plant, a typical one is nearly 12 feet

454
00:25:02,221 --> 00:25:07,120
long, 6 feet high, and weighs close to 3 tons.

455
00:25:07,121 --> 00:25:10,240
Paper from the parent roll is fed into another machine, where

456
00:25:10,241 --> 00:25:15,340
it's wound onto individual 12-foot long cardboard tubes.

457
00:25:15,341 --> 00:25:18,080
Along the way, the paper is stamped at regular intervals

458
00:25:18,081 --> 00:25:22,240
with a sawtooth knife blade, resulting in perforations.

459
00:25:22,241 --> 00:25:26,240
The long rolls of toilet paper, called logs, are removed and then

460
00:25:26,340 --> 00:25:29,180
cut to their familiar size with a precision saw.

461
00:25:29,280 --> 00:25:32,880
It actually runs at such a high speed and is so sharp that it

462
00:25:32,980 --> 00:25:36,720
actually slices through the rolls without mashing or crushing

463
00:25:36,820 --> 00:25:40,720
the cores of the rolls. Today's toilet paper is far

464
00:25:40,820 --> 00:25:43,560
softer than the Scots Victorian-era counterpart.

465
00:25:45,760 --> 00:25:48,340
Modern manufacturers use chemicals to completely

466
00:25:48,440 --> 00:25:51,060
dissolve wood fibers in the pulp mix.

467
00:25:51,061 --> 00:25:54,300
The process was pioneered in the 1940s by the Scots'

468
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:57,860
competitor, Northern Tissue, now Quilted Northern.

469
00:25:57,960 --> 00:26:01,400
Their advertisements referred to their product as splinter-free.

470
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:07,520
Today's factories can produce up to 5 million rolls of toilet

471
00:26:07,620 --> 00:26:08,520
paper every day.

472
00:26:10,720 --> 00:26:12,520
It's a good thing, because Americans

473
00:26:12,620 --> 00:26:15,860
use 26 billion rolls of toilet paper each year,

474
00:26:15,960 --> 00:26:19,460
and that number just keeps on growing.

475
00:26:19,461 --> 00:26:24,580
While rolled toilet paper took only a few years to become established, another novelty

476
00:26:24,581 --> 00:26:28,700
item, the toothbrush, took hundreds of years to catch on.

477
00:26:28,701 --> 00:26:33,940
Next, we brush up on oral hygiene.

478
00:26:33,941 --> 00:26:39,820
The average American uses about 20,805 sheets of toilet paper a year.

479
00:26:39,821 --> 00:26:42,460
Bathroom Tech will continue on Modern Marvels.

480
00:26:49,460 --> 00:26:56,460
You're watching Bathroom Tech on Modern Marvels.

481
00:26:56,461 --> 00:26:59,460
Aside from visiting the toilet,

482
00:26:59,461 --> 00:27:03,460
the second most common act performed in the American bathroom

483
00:27:03,461 --> 00:27:06,460
involves the daily care and maintenance of teeth.

484
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:11,460
Today, there's a dazzling array of choices.

485
00:27:11,461 --> 00:27:14,460
Motorized and manual toothbrushes,

486
00:27:14,461 --> 00:27:16,460
assorted toothpastes and mouthwashes

487
00:27:16,461 --> 00:27:19,300
and mouthwashes in a myriad of colors and flavors.

488
00:27:23,060 --> 00:27:26,180
But the first instrument to clean teeth wasn't a brush.

489
00:27:26,840 --> 00:27:30,680
It was a stick used by ancient Egyptians in 3000 BC.

490
00:27:31,980 --> 00:27:35,240
They actually had a special tree where they break off branches,

491
00:27:35,300 --> 00:27:37,040
and these branches frayed very easily,

492
00:27:37,260 --> 00:27:38,420
and they were called chew sticks.

493
00:27:38,640 --> 00:27:40,420
You would fray the end, chew on it,

494
00:27:40,421 --> 00:27:43,120
and then you'd use the frayed end to clean your teeth.

495
00:27:44,380 --> 00:27:45,460
The chew stick,

496
00:27:45,461 --> 00:27:47,660
along with its smaller cousin, the toothpick,

497
00:27:48,020 --> 00:27:50,060
were the principal tools of oral hygiene

498
00:27:50,061 --> 00:27:51,860
for more than 45 centuries.

499
00:27:53,560 --> 00:27:56,740
But in 1498, the world's first toothbrush

500
00:27:56,741 --> 00:27:57,840
appeared in China.

501
00:27:58,040 --> 00:28:01,440
We don't know if they first used bone as a handle

502
00:28:01,441 --> 00:28:03,560
or a bamboo handle, but we do know

503
00:28:03,561 --> 00:28:06,380
that the bristles were boar hair inserted

504
00:28:06,381 --> 00:28:08,600
into a straight handle at 90 degrees

505
00:28:08,601 --> 00:28:11,420
and would have looked much like brushes do today.

506
00:28:12,560 --> 00:28:14,500
The boar bristles came from the backs

507
00:28:14,501 --> 00:28:17,700
of Siberian hogs. The region's cold climate gave

508
00:28:17,701 --> 00:28:22,060
their hair its proper thickness. Here you have your basic bone

509
00:28:22,061 --> 00:28:24,740
handle toothbrush. The boar's haired bristles have

510
00:28:24,741 --> 00:28:28,940
been inset, and they have also been cut on a curve to adapt them

511
00:28:28,941 --> 00:28:31,460
to the teeth. You can see there are a lot of

512
00:28:31,461 --> 00:28:35,660
bristles in this brush, much larger than a brush today.

513
00:28:35,661 --> 00:28:38,800
And modern angled toothbrushes are nothing new.

514
00:28:38,801 --> 00:28:42,260
In the 19th century, toothbrush handles made from cow bone were

515
00:28:42,261 --> 00:28:45,060
are carved into many different shapes and sizes.

516
00:28:45,061 --> 00:28:47,500
This is a strip of bone that's been cut.

517
00:28:47,501 --> 00:28:51,260
This will serve as a toothbrush handle blank.

518
00:28:51,261 --> 00:28:54,080
The basic shape is carved from the bone

519
00:28:54,081 --> 00:28:55,440
to get your toothbrush shape.

520
00:28:55,441 --> 00:28:59,900
That basic shape is further refined, and this is polished.

521
00:28:59,901 --> 00:29:02,500
And at this point, the holes are drilled

522
00:29:02,501 --> 00:29:04,700
in the head of the brush.

523
00:29:04,701 --> 00:29:08,500
And then the boar's hair is inserted into the holes

524
00:29:08,501 --> 00:29:10,880
and secured with usually copper wire.

525
00:29:10,881 --> 00:29:14,020
And then the boar hair is trimmed to the desired length

526
00:29:14,021 --> 00:29:15,380
for the brush.

527
00:29:15,381 --> 00:29:21,020
And you have a completed boar hair bone handle toothbrush.

528
00:29:21,021 --> 00:29:23,520
Given the painstaking work, it's not surprising

529
00:29:23,521 --> 00:29:26,560
that early toothbrushes were often very expensive.

530
00:29:26,561 --> 00:29:29,000
People generally shared them.

531
00:29:29,001 --> 00:29:32,700
A family may have had one, if they had any at all.

532
00:29:32,701 --> 00:29:36,080
Early in the 20th century, with the advent of plastics,

533
00:29:36,081 --> 00:29:39,040
bone handles were replaced by celluloid ones.

534
00:29:39,041 --> 00:29:41,500
But the boar hair remained.

535
00:29:41,501 --> 00:29:44,980
Animal bristles remained the standard up until 1938,

536
00:29:44,981 --> 00:29:49,080
when the DuPont Chemical Company introduced the first nylon

537
00:29:49,081 --> 00:29:51,180
toothbrush, nylon bristles.

538
00:29:51,181 --> 00:29:54,920
And the benefit was it did not hold bacteria,

539
00:29:54,921 --> 00:29:59,000
and it did not pull out of the brush.

540
00:29:59,001 --> 00:30:03,060
The DuPont Company actually invented nylon in 1938

541
00:30:03,061 --> 00:30:05,440
for use in toothbrush bristles.

542
00:30:05,441 --> 00:30:08,880
But it didn't become widely accepted

543
00:30:08,980 --> 00:30:14,680
until you get into World War II, and the supply of boar hair

544
00:30:14,780 --> 00:30:16,680
is virtually cut off from the United States.

545
00:30:16,780 --> 00:30:19,680
So they had to have something to replace that.

546
00:30:19,780 --> 00:30:23,500
And this something was DuPont's nylon.

547
00:30:23,600 --> 00:30:26,600
The first toothbrush made with synthetic DuPont bristles

548
00:30:26,700 --> 00:30:29,700
was called Dr. West's Miracle Tuft.

549
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:31,800
Others followed.

550
00:30:31,900 --> 00:30:34,100
A typical ad from the early 1940s

551
00:30:34,101 --> 00:30:37,900
featured grateful boards.

552
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,340
Today's nylon bristles are produced much the same way

553
00:30:40,440 --> 00:30:42,240
as they were in the 40s.

554
00:30:42,340 --> 00:30:44,240
First high-speed machines insert the bristles

555
00:30:44,340 --> 00:30:46,440
into the plastic handles.

556
00:30:46,540 --> 00:30:48,540
You heat the handle, create the hole,

557
00:30:48,640 --> 00:30:50,660
insert the tufts into the handle.

558
00:30:50,760 --> 00:30:51,660
That cools down.

559
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:54,660
The head solidifies around the tufts.

560
00:30:54,760 --> 00:30:57,260
You trim them, and then they are polished

561
00:30:57,360 --> 00:31:03,600
so that the ends are not rough and are smooth, rounded edges.

562
00:31:03,601 --> 00:31:05,740
Another revolution in toothbrush technology

563
00:31:05,840 --> 00:31:09,540
came in the 1960s with the arrival of the first electrics.

564
00:31:09,640 --> 00:31:12,480
Brush your teeth with 3,600 up and down

565
00:31:12,580 --> 00:31:14,440
brush strokes a minute.

566
00:31:14,540 --> 00:31:16,640
Since then, microtechnology has allowed

567
00:31:16,740 --> 00:31:21,060
for smaller, more powerful, and cheaper brushes.

568
00:31:21,160 --> 00:31:22,860
That's where the industry's headed,

569
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:27,200
the smaller, disposable, battery-powered electric

570
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:29,960
toothbrushes, although I don't think

571
00:31:29,961 --> 00:31:33,960
manual toothbrushes will ever, at least in my lifetime,

572
00:31:34,060 --> 00:31:35,960
will ever disappear.

573
00:31:36,060 --> 00:31:37,960
Of course, where oral hygiene is concerned,

574
00:31:38,060 --> 00:31:40,900
toothbrushes are only half the story.

575
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:43,900
There's also toothpaste, which, like the chew stick,

576
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:47,520
dates back to the Egyptians.

577
00:31:47,620 --> 00:31:50,820
They made a toothpaste using a ground pumice stone,

578
00:31:50,920 --> 00:31:54,760
like volcanic ash, and they would mix it with a wine

579
00:31:54,860 --> 00:31:58,120
vinegar, something acidic, to actually clean the teeth.

580
00:31:58,121 --> 00:32:01,360
and it worked very, very well.

581
00:32:01,460 --> 00:32:03,160
The first modern tooth cleaning products

582
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:04,660
were tooth powders.

583
00:32:04,760 --> 00:32:07,860
Users had to create their own lather.

584
00:32:07,960 --> 00:32:10,160
You pour it out in your hand, get your brush wet,

585
00:32:10,260 --> 00:32:13,460
dip your brush in your hand, and start brushing your teeth.

586
00:32:13,560 --> 00:32:15,880
Toothpastes arrived during the Victorian era.

587
00:32:15,980 --> 00:32:17,880
They were easier to use because they were already

588
00:32:17,980 --> 00:32:19,580
mixed with water.

589
00:32:19,680 --> 00:32:22,180
Early toothpastes were upscale items sold

590
00:32:22,280 --> 00:32:24,780
in fancy porcelain pots.

591
00:32:24,781 --> 00:32:28,780
In 1892, a Connecticut dentist named Washington Sheffield

592
00:32:28,781 --> 00:32:31,780
became the first to sell toothpaste and collapsible

593
00:32:31,781 --> 00:32:35,780
tubes. Sheffield's son got the million-dollar

594
00:32:35,781 --> 00:32:40,780
idea after visiting an art store and seeing tubes of oil paint.

595
00:32:40,781 --> 00:32:46,780
Dad made it a business. Colgate followed suit in 1896.

596
00:32:46,781 --> 00:32:51,780
And the Colgate way stops tooth decay best.

597
00:32:51,781 --> 00:32:54,420
But Sheffield's innovation continued to squeeze out

598
00:32:54,520 --> 00:32:56,020
profits.

599
00:32:56,120 --> 00:32:58,320
That company is still in existence today.

600
00:32:58,420 --> 00:32:59,820
They make toothpaste.

601
00:32:59,920 --> 00:33:03,120
They make any kind of tube you could possibly think of.

602
00:33:03,220 --> 00:33:04,520
With the introduction of tubes,

603
00:33:04,620 --> 00:33:07,520
toothpaste manufacturers had to develop specialized machines

604
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:09,440
to fill them.

605
00:33:09,540 --> 00:33:12,340
Then as now, paste is injected into the tube.

606
00:33:15,040 --> 00:33:17,540
The bottoms of the tubes are then folded over and heat

607
00:33:17,640 --> 00:33:19,240
sealed.

608
00:33:19,241 --> 00:33:22,040
Modern toothpastes come in pastes and gels,

609
00:33:22,041 --> 00:33:25,240
and in some cases, both. But how do they get the toothpaste

610
00:33:25,241 --> 00:33:28,840
to come out in stripes? Well, they put it in that way,

611
00:33:28,841 --> 00:33:31,880
thanks to the multi-part injector nozzle, which allows them

612
00:33:31,881 --> 00:33:35,480
to fill the tube with 10 distinct streams of dental cream.

613
00:33:35,481 --> 00:33:39,240
Then you just squeeze the tube, and voila,

614
00:33:39,241 --> 00:33:44,240
you've got striped toothpaste. Today, the emphasis isn't just

615
00:33:44,241 --> 00:33:47,240
on cleaning teeth but whitening them.

616
00:33:47,241 --> 00:33:49,740
Special toothpastes and plaque rinses have been formulated

617
00:33:49,741 --> 00:33:51,480
for that purpose.

618
00:33:51,481 --> 00:33:54,540
But the world's first tooth whitening mouthwash was reportedly

619
00:33:54,541 --> 00:33:57,480
developed back in Roman times.

620
00:33:57,481 --> 00:33:59,980
And you'll never guess what was in it.

621
00:33:59,981 --> 00:34:05,300
Catullus, the Roman poet, makes fun of a Roman rival of his,

622
00:34:05,301 --> 00:34:09,300
and he alleged that this man's particular region of Spain,

623
00:34:09,301 --> 00:34:13,900
it was the practice to clean one's teeth by swishing one's

624
00:34:13,901 --> 00:34:17,740
swishing one's mouth out with early morning urine.

625
00:34:17,741 --> 00:34:19,780
But the Romans weren't alone.

626
00:34:19,781 --> 00:34:22,500
16 centuries later, Pierre Fauchard,

627
00:34:22,501 --> 00:34:24,400
the father of modern dentistry, also

628
00:34:24,401 --> 00:34:26,280
advocated cleaning teeth with urine.

629
00:34:26,281 --> 00:34:29,380
He, through experimentation, had realized

630
00:34:29,381 --> 00:34:31,860
that urine had something in it that

631
00:34:31,861 --> 00:34:34,860
was beneficial to the teeth and health of the gums.

632
00:34:34,861 --> 00:34:37,000
The mysterious chemical was ammonia.

633
00:34:37,001 --> 00:34:38,920
Now, if you've ever not cleaned your cat litter box,

634
00:34:38,921 --> 00:34:41,940
you know how pungent the smell of ammonia can be.

635
00:34:41,941 --> 00:34:45,500
And it is the ammonia molecule in the ureic acid

636
00:34:45,501 --> 00:34:46,620
that actually whitens teeth.

637
00:34:46,621 --> 00:34:48,520
And up until modern times, ammonia

638
00:34:48,521 --> 00:34:51,120
was used in all kinds of whitening and toothpaste

639
00:34:51,121 --> 00:34:53,180
products.

640
00:34:53,181 --> 00:34:55,900
During the Middle Ages, people began bleaching their teeth

641
00:34:55,901 --> 00:34:57,660
to whiten them.

642
00:34:57,661 --> 00:35:00,800
The typical thing is you went to your local barber,

643
00:35:00,801 --> 00:35:05,020
and he applied a paste, which was largely bleach.

644
00:35:05,021 --> 00:35:06,940
It definitely whitened your teeth,

645
00:35:06,941 --> 00:35:10,060
but it ate the enamel off.

646
00:35:10,061 --> 00:35:13,800
Over time, the practice led to massive tooth decay.

647
00:35:13,801 --> 00:35:16,520
You had many rulers in Europe who early in their reign

648
00:35:16,521 --> 00:35:19,060
had very white teeth from all the bleaching.

649
00:35:19,061 --> 00:35:21,200
But as their reign progressed, they

650
00:35:21,201 --> 00:35:25,080
were in constant agony, almost demented from the pain,

651
00:35:25,081 --> 00:35:27,120
because there were no dentists to speak of.

652
00:35:27,121 --> 00:35:29,300
There was no one to take care of tooth decay.

653
00:35:29,301 --> 00:35:31,760
You either got someone to knock your tooth out

654
00:35:31,761 --> 00:35:34,940
or to pull it out with a string.

655
00:35:34,941 --> 00:35:38,880
In the centuries to come, milder bleaches were developed.

656
00:35:38,881 --> 00:35:44,560
By the 1990s, dentists began bleaching teeth themselves.

657
00:35:44,561 --> 00:35:47,320
Tooth whitening technologies now progressed to include home

658
00:35:47,321 --> 00:35:48,880
bleaching kits.

659
00:35:48,881 --> 00:35:51,560
Crest white strips, which are plastic strips covered with a

660
00:35:51,561 --> 00:35:57,500
mild bleaching gel, were launched in 2001.

661
00:35:57,501 --> 00:36:00,720
Speaking of launches, have you ever wondered how astronauts go

662
00:36:00,721 --> 00:36:03,040
to the bathroom?

663
00:36:03,041 --> 00:36:06,280
Next, going where no man has flushed before.

664
00:36:08,880 --> 00:36:12,080
During World War II, the British Army shipped toothbrushes

665
00:36:12,081 --> 00:36:15,520
with secret hollow handles to POWs in Germany.

666
00:36:15,521 --> 00:36:18,680
Inside the handles were wafer-thin maps of Germany

667
00:36:18,681 --> 00:36:20,720
to aid in any escape attempt.

668
00:36:20,721 --> 00:36:23,320
Bathroom Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

669
00:36:27,840 --> 00:36:30,840
You're watching Bathroom Tech on Modern Marvels.

670
00:36:34,040 --> 00:36:36,780
Today, Bathroom Tech has become so advanced

671
00:36:36,781 --> 00:36:41,780
that it can seem like science fiction.

672
00:36:41,781 --> 00:36:45,280
The Moen Company has developed high-tech computerized showers

673
00:36:45,281 --> 00:36:47,280
that have more jets than an air show.

674
00:36:50,280 --> 00:36:53,780
In Japan, the Toto Company has developed combination bathtub

675
00:36:53,781 --> 00:36:57,280
saunas, as well as self-cleaning whirlpool baths.

676
00:36:59,780 --> 00:37:02,280
The California company went one step further

677
00:37:02,281 --> 00:37:04,780
and created an entire self-cleaning bathroom.

678
00:37:06,780 --> 00:37:10,120
But to see the true future of bathroom technology, one only

679
00:37:10,220 --> 00:37:13,180
has to look to the sky.

680
00:37:13,280 --> 00:37:18,720
3, 2, 1, 0, and liftoff.

681
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:21,720
Bathroom technology in space has come a long way

682
00:37:21,820 --> 00:37:24,000
from the first flight, when rocket scientists

683
00:37:24,100 --> 00:37:26,660
seemed to think of everything except what to do

684
00:37:26,760 --> 00:37:29,740
if an astronaut had to go to the bathroom.

685
00:37:29,840 --> 00:37:31,940
That was something that they didn't even account

686
00:37:32,040 --> 00:37:34,280
for in their design.

687
00:37:34,281 --> 00:37:37,520
And when Alan Shepard first went up in the space capsule,

688
00:37:37,620 --> 00:37:41,460
it was a short trip, but he had to go to the bathroom on launch.

689
00:37:41,560 --> 00:37:43,360
And rather than delay the launch,

690
00:37:43,460 --> 00:37:46,220
all of the engineers came together,

691
00:37:46,320 --> 00:37:49,020
thought about what it would do, and told him just

692
00:37:49,120 --> 00:37:50,060
to go in his pants.

693
00:37:54,100 --> 00:37:58,800
So he made the trip through space in wet pants, but survived.

694
00:37:58,900 --> 00:38:01,640
And after that, they started thinking about waste management.

695
00:38:04,280 --> 00:38:07,320
NASA's first official bathroom technology developed for the

696
00:38:07,321 --> 00:38:10,820
Mercury, Gemini, and Apollo missions was definitely low

697
00:38:10,821 --> 00:38:12,420
tech.

698
00:38:12,421 --> 00:38:15,520
Astronauts were given plastic bags.

699
00:38:15,521 --> 00:38:19,220
There was a bag for urination, and there was a bag for

700
00:38:19,221 --> 00:38:20,720
defecation.

701
00:38:20,721 --> 00:38:23,640
And I would coin them the pee and poo pouches of the space

702
00:38:23,641 --> 00:38:25,440
world.

703
00:38:25,441 --> 00:38:27,740
Engineers soon realized the bags weren't

704
00:38:27,741 --> 00:38:29,140
a permanent solution.

705
00:38:29,141 --> 00:38:30,680
They often leaked.

706
00:38:30,681 --> 00:38:33,380
And there was a bigger problem.

707
00:38:33,381 --> 00:38:37,820
Body waste in space actually tend to stick with you.

708
00:38:37,920 --> 00:38:40,780
That's why I kind of use a friendly term of sticky.

709
00:38:40,880 --> 00:38:45,720
But you have to overcome that force.

710
00:38:45,820 --> 00:38:47,720
When the space shuttle was on the drawing boards,

711
00:38:47,820 --> 00:38:52,040
NASA engineers knew that they had to create a working toilet.

712
00:38:52,140 --> 00:38:54,500
They had to address not just the environment of space,

713
00:38:54,600 --> 00:38:55,840
but human anatomy as well.

714
00:38:59,580 --> 00:39:03,140
The toilet they came up with had two parts, a sit-down commode

715
00:39:03,141 --> 00:39:08,740
solid waste and a handheld urinal to take care of liquids.

716
00:39:08,840 --> 00:39:13,620
Both commode and urinal use suction to remove the waste.

717
00:39:13,720 --> 00:39:16,020
We call it air entrainment.

718
00:39:16,120 --> 00:39:18,260
Basically, you're sitting on the inlet to a fan,

719
00:39:18,360 --> 00:39:20,260
if you ever can kind of conceive of this,

720
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:26,860
of taking your vacuum cleaner at home and kind of sitting on it.

721
00:39:26,960 --> 00:39:29,740
Calibrating the precise level of suction was tricky,

722
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:31,600
especially in the urinal.

723
00:39:31,601 --> 00:39:35,000
We have an airflow that is gentle enough to remove the

724
00:39:35,080 --> 00:39:38,940
liquid from your body and pull it down the tube without getting

725
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:41,940
back into the cabin, because obviously you would not want

726
00:39:42,020 --> 00:39:44,040
to have the little yellow raindrops floating through

727
00:39:44,120 --> 00:39:46,240
the cabin.

728
00:39:46,320 --> 00:39:48,920
For sanitation purposes, each astronaut is assigned

729
00:39:48,980 --> 00:39:51,860
his or her own urinal funnel.

730
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:55,020
We do have a female funnel, and this will now fit the

731
00:39:55,100 --> 00:39:56,360
proper geometry.

732
00:39:56,420 --> 00:40:00,160
Or basically, you can even stand up for your rights in space.

733
00:40:00,220 --> 00:40:01,600
The ladies can.

734
00:40:01,601 --> 00:40:06,600
The air used by the toilet's fans had to be recycled as well.

735
00:40:06,601 --> 00:40:10,600
Physically, it has to go through a downstream fan and then goes

736
00:40:10,601 --> 00:40:13,600
through an odor bacteria filter, and voila, comes back into the

737
00:40:13,601 --> 00:40:18,600
cabin, and you've got nice fresh air again, hopefully.

738
00:40:18,601 --> 00:40:21,600
Zero gravity also meant that something was needed to help

739
00:40:21,601 --> 00:40:25,600
anchor the astronauts to the toilet.

740
00:40:25,601 --> 00:40:28,600
Designers solved this problem with the addition of thigh bars

741
00:40:28,601 --> 00:40:31,500
that swivel across the lap.

742
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:34,240
And while astronauts still use toilet paper,

743
00:40:34,340 --> 00:40:35,980
they must use it sparingly.

744
00:40:36,080 --> 00:40:38,080
You'd wipe yourself, put some of the crucial wipes

745
00:40:38,180 --> 00:40:40,840
in the bowl, and then you would put the extra wipes

746
00:40:40,940 --> 00:40:43,740
in another little receptacle and put it in the trash later on.

747
00:40:46,860 --> 00:40:47,620
1,000.

748
00:40:47,720 --> 00:40:50,180
Each time the shuttle lands, the space commode

749
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:52,720
is full and must be serviced.

750
00:40:52,820 --> 00:40:55,660
Since this arrangement wouldn't be possible on a permanent

751
00:40:55,661 --> 00:40:58,700
structure like the space station, NASA commissioned

752
00:40:58,800 --> 00:41:00,400
the next generation of space toilet.

753
00:41:03,400 --> 00:41:06,500
This model uses individual bags beneath the seat.

754
00:41:06,600 --> 00:41:09,300
The bags are constructed from a Teflon coated plastic that

755
00:41:09,400 --> 00:41:12,880
allows air to pass through, but not liquids or solids.

756
00:41:12,980 --> 00:41:15,120
I will now insert a bag, but you

757
00:41:15,220 --> 00:41:19,720
can see the airflow pulls the bag down and deploys it,

758
00:41:19,820 --> 00:41:20,960
and we set it right there.

759
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:25,660
OK, now you'll put the seat down.

760
00:41:25,661 --> 00:41:27,900
and you're ready to use it.

761
00:41:28,000 --> 00:41:29,600
So you float over to it.

762
00:41:29,700 --> 00:41:32,460
Again, you grab the thigh bars and hold yourself down.

763
00:41:32,560 --> 00:41:35,100
Otherwise, you may have a premature liftoff from the seat.

764
00:41:35,200 --> 00:41:36,600
You want to make sure you stay right here

765
00:41:36,700 --> 00:41:38,900
while you're doing your thing.

766
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:40,400
When the astronaut is finished,

767
00:41:40,500 --> 00:41:43,520
he or she covers the bag with a special Tupperware lid.

768
00:41:43,620 --> 00:41:47,720
What you do now is you put it back into the compaction mode,

769
00:41:47,820 --> 00:41:51,020
push the compaction button, and there's a piston here

770
00:41:51,120 --> 00:41:54,360
that'll squeeze the bag down into little people patties

771
00:41:54,361 --> 00:41:56,860
at the bottom of the container.

772
00:41:56,960 --> 00:41:58,960
And being a good neighbor, you come over

773
00:41:59,060 --> 00:42:02,900
and put a fresh bag in for the next use.

774
00:42:03,000 --> 00:42:03,900
OK.

775
00:42:04,000 --> 00:42:05,900
You put the seat down.

776
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:10,400
And also, remember, guys, put the lid down.

777
00:42:10,500 --> 00:42:12,820
When the container is full, one lucky crew member

778
00:42:12,920 --> 00:42:15,220
is assigned the task of removing it and transferring it

779
00:42:15,320 --> 00:42:18,820
to the spacecraft's waste receptacle.

780
00:42:18,920 --> 00:42:20,920
The canisters can then be brought back to Earth

781
00:42:21,020 --> 00:42:23,520
with the rest of the trash.

782
00:42:23,521 --> 00:42:25,180
For now, the space shuttle's commode

783
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:28,220
is still taking care of business the old-fashioned way,

784
00:42:28,320 --> 00:42:29,660
providing astronauts with at least one

785
00:42:29,760 --> 00:42:30,960
of the comforts of home.

786
00:42:34,260 --> 00:42:37,060
It's a different story for spacewalks.

787
00:42:37,160 --> 00:42:40,000
Astronauts must don suits and go outside for up to eight

788
00:42:40,100 --> 00:42:43,240
hours at a time without a bathroom break.

789
00:42:43,340 --> 00:42:45,400
How do they get around this?

790
00:42:45,500 --> 00:42:49,080
Right now, all the men and the women both wear diapers.

791
00:42:49,180 --> 00:42:52,180
Diapers in space actually work better than on the ground.

792
00:42:52,181 --> 00:42:54,720
Because in space, really, nothing drips,

793
00:42:54,820 --> 00:42:57,220
because there's no gravity.

794
00:42:57,320 --> 00:43:00,080
Shuttle designers also had to address ordinary bathroom

795
00:43:00,180 --> 00:43:01,520
hygiene as well.

796
00:43:01,620 --> 00:43:02,920
Given the weightless environment,

797
00:43:03,020 --> 00:43:05,820
showers wouldn't be practical.

798
00:43:05,920 --> 00:43:08,900
The solution is body washes and shampoos that

799
00:43:09,000 --> 00:43:11,840
can be toweled off rather than rinsed.

800
00:43:11,940 --> 00:43:13,940
For each day in orbit, an astronaut

801
00:43:14,040 --> 00:43:19,240
is allowed two washcloths and one towel for personal use.

802
00:43:19,340 --> 00:43:21,640
And since spitting in space would be problematic,

803
00:43:21,641 --> 00:43:25,880
They had to develop toothpastes that the astronauts could swallow.

804
00:43:25,881 --> 00:43:28,840
Of course, communal living aboard the shuttle requires everyone

805
00:43:28,841 --> 00:43:31,380
to pitch in.

806
00:43:31,381 --> 00:43:34,380
There's no janitor there to physically clean the bathroom

807
00:43:34,381 --> 00:43:36,680
for you. So I like to tell young kids,

808
00:43:36,681 --> 00:43:39,400
go home tonight and do some early astronaut training.

809
00:43:39,401 --> 00:43:41,600
Help your mom and dad clean the bathroom at home,

810
00:43:41,601 --> 00:43:45,000
because someday you may be doing it yourself.

811
00:43:45,001 --> 00:43:47,500
In the space of 200 years,

812
00:43:47,501 --> 00:43:50,840
bathroom technology has evolved from holes in the earth

813
00:43:50,841 --> 00:43:56,080
to a computer-driven commode that orbits 200 miles above it.

814
00:43:56,180 --> 00:43:59,580
And if human beings ever plumb distant galaxies,

815
00:43:59,680 --> 00:44:01,380
one thing's for sure.

816
00:44:01,480 --> 00:44:05,080
Their plumbing will be right there with them.

